From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:25:08 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/sun4i: drv: Do not call drm_mode_config_cleanup()

Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S: unbinding the DisplayPort transmitter
oopses in drm_mode_config_cleanup(). Removing the component unbinds the
display engine, whose unbind callback calls drm_mode_config_cleanup()
by hand. That calls encoder->funcs->destroy() for every encoder, and the
encoder of the transmitter is a managed one (drmm_encoder_init()) with
no funcs, so it dereferences NULL. The bind error path calls it the same
way, so every failed bind did the same. The connector of the transmitter
(drm_bridge_connector_init()) is managed as well and has no destroy.

drm_mode_config_init() is managed: it registers
drm_mode_config_cleanup() as a managed action, which runs when the last
reference to the drm_device is dropped. Managed encoders, connectors,
planes and CRTCs clean up before it and are gone from its lists by then.
The explicit calls are redundant and clash with them, so remove them,
and call drmm_mode_config_init() and check its result.

The explicit call also clashed with sun4i_tv, which cleans up its
connector and encoder in its unbind callback: that ran a second time on
objects the call had already destroyed and cleared. Without the explicit
call the cleanup of sun4i_tv runs once.

The managed release runs after component_unbind_all(), and, while
userspace holds the device open, after the display engine device has
released its devres. What is still on the lists then is destroyed
through its .destroy hook, so it must be in memory that is still there.
The DSI encoder and connector are, they live in the memory of the probe,
and so is the dw-hdmi connector, which the bridge reference from the
previous patch keeps. The following patches make the rest managed: the
HDMI encoder, which lives in memory of the HDMI device that
component_unbind_all() frees, then the CRTC, the planes, the RGB and
LVDS outputs and the dw-hdmi encoder. What is left on the lists at the
end has a .destroy hook and memory that outlives the release.

Not covered: unbinding the display engine with the DRM device held open
by userspace while the HDMI or DisplayPort device is removed too.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: the DisplayPort device unbound and bound
again three times with a monitor attached, without an oops; the picture
returns after each bind.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_drv.c | 10 +++++-----
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_drv.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_drv.c
index c60861cf05ad..e767a90ebe23 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun4i/sun4i_drv.c
@@ -87,12 +87,14 @@ static int sun4i_drv_bind(struct device *dev)
 		goto free_drm;
 	}
 
-	drm_mode_config_init(drm);
+	ret = drmm_mode_config_init(drm);
+	if (ret)
+		goto release_mem;
 
 	ret = component_bind_all(drm->dev, drm);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(drm->dev, "Couldn't bind all pipelines components\n");
-		goto cleanup_mode_config;
+		goto release_mem;
 	}
 
 	/* drm_vblank_init calls kcalloc, which can fail */
@@ -124,8 +126,7 @@ static int sun4i_drv_bind(struct device *dev)
 	drm_kms_helper_poll_fini(drm);
 unbind_all:
 	component_unbind_all(dev, NULL);
-cleanup_mode_config:
-	drm_mode_config_cleanup(drm);
+release_mem:
 	of_reserved_mem_device_release(dev);
 free_drm:
 	drm_dev_put(drm);
@@ -140,7 +141,6 @@ static void sun4i_drv_unbind(struct device *dev)
 	drm_dev_unregister(drm);
 	drm_kms_helper_poll_fini(drm);
 	drm_atomic_helper_shutdown(drm);
-	drm_mode_config_cleanup(drm);
 
 	component_unbind_all(dev, NULL);
 	of_reserved_mem_device_release(dev);
